1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,851
Welcome to Module 6!

2
00:00:01,851 --> 00:00:07,460
Here, we'll look at Opportunities, where
you track every deal from first enquiry to

3
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:08,541
paying customer.

4
00:00:08,541 --> 00:00:13,069
In this lesson, we'll cover the
building blocks: pipelines, stages,

5
00:00:13,069 --> 00:00:14,420
values and statuses.

6
00:00:15,272 --> 00:00:17,254
First, a contact is a person.

7
00:00:17,254 --> 00:00:21,081
An opportunity is a deal you
might win with that person.

8
00:00:21,081 --> 00:00:22,994
Olivia Bennett is a contact.

9
00:00:22,994 --> 00:00:26,274
Olivia's Invisalign
treatment is an opportunity.

10
00:00:26,274 --> 00:00:29,896
One contact can have several
opportunities over time.

11
00:00:30,747 --> 00:00:35,429
A pipeline is your sales process,
laid out as stages from left to right.

12
00:00:35,429 --> 00:00:40,696
This one, called Patient Journey, follows
a new patient from their first enquiry,

13
00:00:40,696 --> 00:00:42,777
all the way to booked treatment.

14
00:00:43,627 --> 00:00:45,124
Each column is a stage.

15
00:00:45,124 --> 00:00:49,941
New enquiry, contacted, consultation
booked, treatment plan sent, and won.

16
00:00:49,941 --> 00:00:54,107
As a deal moves forward, its card
moves one column to the right.

17
00:00:54,107 --> 00:00:59,509
Put your stages in the order things really
happen, so the board reads like a story,

18
00:00:59,509 --> 00:01:00,746
from left to right.

19
00:01:01,595 --> 00:01:06,425
The top of each column shows how
many deals are in that stage,

20
00:01:06,425 --> 00:01:08,216
and what they're worth.

21
00:01:08,216 --> 00:01:11,800
New Enquiry has 3
opportunities, worth £7,690.

22
00:01:12,649 --> 00:01:14,575
Each card is one opportunity.

23
00:01:14,575 --> 00:01:19,424
It shows the deal name, the contact,
where the lead came from, the value,

24
00:01:19,424 --> 00:01:20,487
and who owns it.

25
00:01:20,487 --> 00:01:26,000
The icons along the bottom let you call,
text or email them, or add a task or note,

26
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,727
without leaving the board.

27
00:01:28,579 --> 00:01:31,824
Always add a value, even
if it's an estimate.

28
00:01:31,824 --> 00:01:35,645
Values are what turn a list
of names into a forecast.

29
00:01:35,645 --> 00:01:40,187
If you don't know the exact amount
yet, use your average price,

30
00:01:40,187 --> 00:01:42,927
and update it once the plan is agreed.

31
00:01:42,927 --> 00:01:46,749
Here, everything in this
pipeline adds up to £24,740.

32
00:01:47,599 --> 00:01:50,036
Every opportunity also has a status.

33
00:01:50,036 --> 00:01:52,609
Open means you're still working on it.

34
00:01:52,609 --> 00:01:54,234
Won means they said yes.

35
00:01:54,234 --> 00:01:55,859
Lost means they said no.

36
00:01:55,859 --> 00:02:00,665
And abandoned means they went quiet,
or you've decided to stop chasing.

37
00:02:01,516 --> 00:02:05,641
Here's the part people often miss:
stage and status are separate.

38
00:02:05,641 --> 00:02:09,386
Dragging a card into the Won
column doesn't mark it as won.

39
00:02:09,386 --> 00:02:11,988
Open the card, and set its status to Won,

40
00:02:11,988 --> 00:02:14,908
because the status is
what your reports count.

41
00:02:15,759 --> 00:02:20,422
When you mark a deal as lost, choose
a reason, like price, or no response.

42
00:02:20,422 --> 00:02:25,274
After a few months, those reasons tell
you exactly what to fix in your offer,

43
00:02:25,274 --> 00:02:26,409
or your follow-up.

44
00:02:27,263 --> 00:02:31,205
You can also run separate
pipelines for different services.

45
00:02:31,205 --> 00:02:35,748
The Starter plan includes one
pipeline, Growth includes up to three,

46
00:02:35,748 --> 00:02:37,151
and Pro is unlimited.

47
00:02:37,151 --> 00:02:41,093
Here, Invisalign has its own
pipeline, with its own stages.

48
00:02:41,946 --> 00:02:43,024
Here's a pro tip.

49
00:02:43,024 --> 00:02:47,207
Create a new pipeline only when
the steps are genuinely different,

50
00:02:47,207 --> 00:02:50,693
like a long Invisalign journey
versus a quick check-up.

51
00:02:50,693 --> 00:02:52,785
Don't create one per team member.

52
00:02:52,785 --> 00:02:55,194
Use the owner filter for that instead.

53
00:02:56,043 --> 00:02:57,931
Now you know how pipelines work.

54
00:02:57,931 --> 00:03:00,822
In the next lesson, we'll
build one from scratch,

55
00:03:00,822 --> 00:03:03,064
and put its stages in the right order.

56
00:03:03,064 --> 00:03:03,890
See you there!
